{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1105651-paradox-and-or-disbelief-vs-blatant-effects","id":1105651,"title":"Paradox and/or disbelief vs blatant Effects","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-20T04:50:00-07:00","id":1105651,"title":"Paradox and/or disbelief vs blatant Effects","content":"Well, let's say your Mage does some blatant vulgar Effect that lasts for a non n\u00e9gligeable amount of time. He/she could fly above a whole city for exemple. <br /> <br /> Let's say he/she does the effect in a safe place where the effect is vulgar without witnesses. It costs a paradox point only if the effect is a success, which is our assumption here. What happens when someone sees the Mage flying? Do you add any mechanics to that? Like pattern bleeding, disbelief (as counter Magick?) or more paradox? <br /> <br /> More over I play with the rule authorizing permanent effects. What about permanent vulgar effects then? Ex: one of my players play a Verbena who made her saliva corrosive. Permanently. Well, it's not a life 5 vulgar-pattern-modification effect. Just a life 3 effect made permanent with many successes. I did not count more paradox than the usual one point cause of its vulgar nature. What would you have done? Was that a mistake? I think it opened the wrong door. Now my players like these kind of effects well... They like them too much <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8572-nicotheduck","name":"NicoTheDuck","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T05:49:48-07:00","id":1105657,"title":"","content":"They become a blatant threat to the consensus and find that the man is not amused.<br /> <br /> Secondly. Permadox. Remember that each permanent vulgar effect is granting at least one permadox.<br /> <br /> Thirdly, roll for backlash. Don't add paradox. Just hit them with backlash when they fly through London doing their Voldemort impression. With aforementioned permadox, the effects can be nasty indeed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T06:02:30-07:00","id":1105661,"title":"","content":"Disbelief and pattern bleeding are very close cousins. Each day the effect(Disbelief) or the subject(pattern bleeding) lose 1 success/health level.<br /> You can circumvent that with quint on a 1:1 basis.<br /> <br /> Disbelief is also a non-issue if you are in a fitting reality zone or alternatively if there are no witnesses.<br /> <br /> If you really want to play up the dying magick aspect you can combine the two.<br /> Each day your magically flying mage is seen flying he gets 1 damage and his effect looses potency.<br /> <br /> Combined with the usual Permadox and the backlash idea it is not a nobrainer to make magick permanent.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T06:13:51-07:00","id":1105664,"title":"","content":"As far as I'm aware, the presence of witnesses does not retroactively make a vulgar effect any worse. It only seems to matter if witnesses were around the moment the Effect was cast. In 2nd Ed and M20, you get a single Paradox point for Vulgar, regardless of who saw it. So it wouldn't matter on that front. Witnesses only really affect the difficulty to cast (well, that and how much 'Dox you get if you Botch, but that's another matter). So long as the Effect is already cast, the successes you got don't go down as a result of a retroactively higher difficulty number. That would make book-keeping a headache.<br /> <br /> That said, Disbelief from witnesses can cause an Effect to unweave. Which might reduce duration or effectiveness, per the Storyteller's judgement. But to my knowledge, Disbelief hasn't really had any solid game mechanics. It is, indeed, entirely up to the Storyteller to decide if people seeing it going on has an adverse effect on the magick.<br /> <br /> Of course, a pity the player whose storyteller decided to negate the PC's flight, while hundreds of feet in the air. Seems like a dick move to me, though there is something to be said of a PC needing to take rapid action to arrest their fall, either by casting another Effect or doing some kind of maneuver.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T07:18:58-07:00","id":1105677,"title":"","content":"I guess it depends on the pyrotechnic aspects of your campaign. I mean, if you run Michael Bay kind of game, you definitely want to put your players in that sort of situations ^^ <br /> <br /> I guess I will try to Houserule the mechanics of disbelief using a few unweaving rolls. It adds randomness to something that is never totally fixed (that is consensus) and prevents the players to do stupid actions while giving them the time to react (long time effects need lots of successes and unweaving will only remove them a few at a time). That way, the flying Mage will begin to glide before falling like a stone or doing more Magick to keep him up in the air (with the possible consequences of more vulgar effects of course). <br /> <br /> What do you think?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8572-nicotheduck","name":"NicoTheDuck","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}